\ (b)~Resulting index perturbation $\Delta n(x)$.
Integral yields $\Delta\phi\approx1.48$\,rad.}
\label{fig:profile}
\end{figure}

\section{Standard Optics Interpretation (SAT-free)}
Consider a thin anisotropic layer whose optic axis rotates
from $0$ to $2\pi/3$ over \SI{2}{\micro\metre}.
If the extraordinary–ordinary index contrast is $\eta=0.02$,
the path‐integrated retardance at \SI{650}{\nano\metre} is
\[
\Delta\phi=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}
\int_{-10\,\mu\mathrm{m}}^{+10\,\mu\mathrm{m}}\!\!\!
\eta\sin^{2}\!\theta(x)\,dx
\simeq1.48\;\mathrm{rad}\;(\approx0.24\lambda).
\]
This value lies comfortably within the detection range of a simple
Mach–Zehnder interferometer equipped with a DSLR camera
and linear–circular polarizers.
No exotic materials are required; a photo‐aligned liquid‐crystal cell
or a stack of rotated polymer waveplates matching
$\theta(x)\!\propto\!\tanh(\mu x)$ suffices.

\section{Experimental Outlook}
A DIY interferometer with \SI{1}{\milli\radian} phase sensitivity
can falsify this prediction:
if the measured retardance lies outside $0.1$–$0.3$\,rad for the
specified $\eta$ and $\mu$, the underlying model is ruled out in
this sector.

